.TH GETPID 2
.SH NAME
getpid, getppid  \-  get process identification
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <unistd.h>
.PP
.B pid_t getpid(void);
.PP
.B pid_t getppid(void);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Getpid
returns
the process ID of
the current process;
.I getppid
that of its parent.
Most often it is used to generate
uniquely-named
temporary files.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
mktemp(3), fork(2)
.SH BESM-6 NOTES
.B One extracode, two entry points.
The gate returns the process id in the accumulator and the
.I parent's
in r12, so
.I getpid
and
.I getppid
are two labels on one stub \- which is why it is hand-written rather than
generated.
.IR Getuid (2)
and
.IR getgid (2)
have the same shape.
.PP
r12 is an index register and so only 15 bits wide.  That does not bite here \-
process ids are small \- but it does in
.IR wait (2).
.SH ASSEMBLER
.B Note:
v7's page gave the PDP-11 form here \- a decimal call number and a
`\fBsys\fP \fIname\fP; \fIarg\fP ...' line.  Neither is how a call is made on this
machine.  The gate is one extracode \-
.B $77 SYS_getpid
\- and the C calling convention has already put the arguments where it reads them:
1..n\-1 below r15, the last in the accumulator, the result in the accumulator, the
error number in r14.
.PP
.B The numbering itself is v7's, unchanged.
The macro is defined in <sys/syscall.h>, which is the only place a syscall number
appears on the user side.  See
.IR intro (2)
for the whole contract.
